<h1>doubly linked list</h1>
<p>
(data structure)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A variant of a <a href="linkedList.html"><em>linked list</em></a> in which each item has a <a href="link.html"><em>link</em></a> to the previous item as well as the next.  This allows easily accessing list items backward as well as forward and deleting any item in constant time.
</p>

<p>
<strong>Also known as</strong> two-way linked list, symmetrically linked list.
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="jumpList.html"><em>jump list</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
See <a href="../terms.html#Stand98">[Stand98, p. 91]</a>. </p> 
<p> <a href="binarySearch.html"><em>Binary search</em></a> may be effective with an  <a href="orderedLinkedList.html"><em>ordered</em></a>, <a href="doublyLinkedList.html"><em>doubly linked list</em></a>. It makes O(n) traversals, as does <a href="linearSearch.html"><em>linear search</em></a>, but it only performs O(log n) comparisons.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

